{"name":"Linoleum Blue","hex":"#427c9d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"linoleum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inexpensive waterproof covering used especially for floors, made from solidified linseed oil over a burlap or canvas backing, or from its modern replacement, polyvinyl chloride."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/linoleum"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Linoleum","blocks":[{"text":"Linoleum is a floor covering made from materials such as solidified linseed oil (linoxyn), pine resin, ground cork dust, sawdust, and mineral fillers such as calcium carbonate, most commonly on a hessian or canvas backing. Pigments are often added to the materials to create the desired color finish. Due to the pigment being mixed directly into the linoleum cement, the finished color of the tile remains visible even after years of wear. In the past some of these pigments were iron oxide, red lead or lithopone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Linoleum_003.jpg/330px-Linoleum_003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linoleum","wikidata":"Q108728"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg/500px-Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg/1280px-Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg/500px-Aanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Application of liquid linoleum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAanbrengen_vloeibaar_linoleum..jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg/500px-Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg/1280px-Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg/500px-Tyntesfield_2015_087.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early linoleum at Tyntesfield","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi